= Arrival

In February I came to the island of Gull Holm with two suitcases and a crate of books. The old keeper had left
the key for me under the rain barrel, with a note: "The lamp wants to be greeted every evening."#footnote[The note
hangs framed in the duty room today, right next to the barometer.]

== The First Morning

I woke up because it was quiet. No wind, no water against the wall, only the ticking of the clock above the
stairs. There are a hundred and twelve steps up to the lantern; that morning I counted them for the first time and
got the number wrong twice.

#scene[Up there the window stood open. On the sill lay a feather, grey with a white edge.]

== The Logbook

In the duty room there is a book with an oilcloth cover. Every page has four columns: day, wind, visibility,
remarks. The remarks of the last thirty years would fit on a postcard. I resolved to keep it differently.

On the flyleaf, in my predecessor's hand, there is a single sentence:

#quote(block: true, attribution: [Flyleaf of the logbook, undated])[Whoever keeps the light writes down what they
see, and not what they fear.]

=== First Entry

Wind north-west, four. Visibility good. Remarks: I am here.

=== What Goes in the Logbook

- the wind, by direction and strength
- the visibility as far as the buoy at the creek
- every ship that passes the island
- and last, in the fourth column, whatever belongs nowhere else

= Summer

In June it does not get dark here, only blue. The lamp burns all the same, because the regulations want it, and
because a lighthouse that sleeps is no lighthouse.

== A Visitor from the Mainland

On Midsummer Day the supply boat came with flour, paraffin and a boy called Ole, who was to stay for three weeks.
On the first evening he asked whether the light shone for the fish as well.

On the second evening he wanted to know who lights the lamp when I am ill. I said: the keeper on South Holm, if he
sees it in time. Ole thought that was not a good answer.

== What the Light Does Not Show

A light shows where you must not go. Where you ought to go, everyone has to know for themselves. I did not tell
Ole that; I wrote it in the logbook.

Above the door to the lantern someone cut a verse into the wood a long time ago:
"Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path" (Psalm 119:105). For half a year I took it for a
service instruction.

= Autumn

The storms come in October, as reliable as the birds of passage. You hear them an hour before you see them.

== The Night Without the Lamp

In the night before the eleventh the wick broke. For four hours I stood at the window with the hand lantern and
swung it, just as the service book of 1911 describes. Nobody was out at sea. But I only knew that in the morning.

== Farewell

Whoever has lit a light every evening for a year never lights anything thoughtlessly again. I left the key under
the rain barrel. I took the note with me.
